  <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
    <name>ADCValue</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):CH$(CH):ADC_100MS</pv_name>
    <x>150</x>
    <y>62</y>
    <width>170</width>
    <height>34</height>
    <format>3</format>
    <precision>6</precision>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):CH$(CH):ADC_100MS (CH$(CH)_ADC_100MS)

ADC integral of channel $(CH) averaged over the last 100 ms, in calibrated
units. This is the value the interlock compares against the ADC thresholds.</tooltip>
    <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
  </widget>
  <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
    <name>FBRHeader</name>
    <text>FBR</text>
    <x>350</x>
    <y>65</y>
    <width>70</width>
    <height>28</height>
    <font>
      <font family="Liberation Sans" style="BOLD" size="18.0">
      </font>
    </font>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
    <name>FBRValue</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):CH$(CH):FBR_CNT_100MS</pv_name>
    <x>480</x>
    <y>62</y>
    <width>170</width>
    <height>34</height>
    <format>3</format>
    <precision>6</precision>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):CH$(CH):FBR_CNT_100MS (CH$(CH)_FBR_CNT_100MS)

Fiber count of channel $(CH) accumulated over the last 100 ms. This is the
value the interlock compares against the FBR thresholds.</tooltip>
    <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
    <name>ADCFilter</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):CH$(CH):ADC_FILT_TH</pv_name>
    <x>610</x>
    <y>130</y>
    <width>282</width>
    <height>34</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):CH$(CH):ADC_FILT_TH (ADC_FILT_TH_CH$(CH))

Noise clamp of channel $(CH), not a filter length: every sample whose signed
value is at or below this level is zeroed before the integrals are formed, so
it keeps the baseline out of the longer time scales. Entered in mV and
converted to raw counts with the inverse calibration; the PL compares that
count as signed 16-bit, so a level outside +/-32767 counts is rejected and
this field returns to its previous value (about +/-1365 mV before
calibration). 0 (the default) already zeroes every non-positive sample: a
channel sitting on a negative baseline therefore reads exactly 0 until the
clamp is set negative.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="label" version="2.0.0">
    <name>ADCFilterLabel</name>
    <text>ADC filter threshold</text>
    <x>670</x>
    <y>164</y>
    <width>180</width>
    <height>22</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textentry" version="3.0.0">
    <name>DACSP</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):CH$(CH):DAC_REF_SP</pv_name>
    <x>910</x>
    <y>62</y>
    <width>120</width>
    <height>34</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):CH$(CH):DAC_REF_SP (DAC_REF_CH$(CH))

Comparator reference of channel $(CH) as a user quantity x, not a DAC voltage.
The driver sends K*x + B (mV) to the matching DAC80508 output over SPI, using
this channel's DAC_CALI_K/DAC_CALI_B coefficients, so a change here or to
either coefficient takes effect immediately.

The front end inverts the negative detector pulse and amplifies it, so the
comparator sees a positive-going signal and weighs it against this level:
raising the reference makes the channel less sensitive. The FPGA interlock
thresholds are a separate, digital comparison.

The range check applies to the transformed value, so a K*x + B outside 0 to
2500 mV is refused (reject ... retained) and this field returns to its
previous x with WRITE_ALARM; the DAC holds the last accepted reference.</tooltip>
  </widget>
  <widget type="textupdate" version="2.0.0">
    <name>DACRBV</name>
    <pv_name>$(P):CH$(CH):DAC_REF_RBV</pv_name>
    <x>1040</x>
    <y>62</y>
    <width>120</width>
    <height>34</height>
    <horizontal_alignment>1</horizontal_alignment>
    <vertical_alignment>1</vertical_alignment>
    <tooltip>$(P):CH$(CH):DAC_REF_RBV (DAC_REF_CH$(CH)_RB)

Reference voltage of channel $(CH) read back from the DAC80508, in mV, so it
reports what the comparator actually sees. On a healthy write this equals the
K*x + B the driver computed from DAC_REF_SP and the calibration coefficients.
An out-of-range K*x + B is refused before it reaches the chip, so the readback
stays on the previous accepted value rather than a clamped one; any other
mismatch means the SPI write did not reach the chip.</tooltip>
    <border_alarm_sensitive>false</border_alarm_sensitive>
  </widget>
